master
22:29[pjb@thalassa org.xquartz:0 bocl 18Gi]$

======================================================================
DIAGNOSIS & RESOLUTION (v1.4.32)
--------------------------------
The module SYSTEM is not broken.  Verified end-to-end in a worktree:

- REQUIRE / PROVIDE and the boot-time module scan work.  At boot the prelude
  scans <prelude-dir>/modules/*/module.manifest into BOCL:*MODULE-LIST*; REQUIRE
  looks a module up there by its manifest :NAME (case-insensitive) and LOADs its
  files.  A dummy module dropped in <prelude-dir>/modules/ is found and loaded.
- With the ASDF module actually built and present, (require :asdf) SUCCEEDS:
  asdf.lisp loads and "ASDF" is pushed on *MODULES*.  It is just SLOW in the
  interpreter (~4-5 min to load the 14k-line single-file asdf.lisp).

Why the user hit UNDEFINED-MODULE-ERROR: the ASDF module was not built/installed
on that machine, so no modules/asdf/module.manifest existed to scan.  ASDF is
built from the modules/asdf-src git submodule (gitlink + .gitmodules ARE committed
at master, pinned to 91dacfd) via `make modules`, then `make install` copies
modules/asdf/ next to the prelude.  A plain `make build && make install` that
skips the submodule init silently produces NO asdf module (the modules/Makefile
asdf target no-ops when asdf-src/ is absent) — and the failure was then a
contentless "#<UNDEFINED-MODULE-ERROR>" with no message, a dead end.

Fix shipped:
- bocl::undefined-module-error now has a :REPORT (it printed as a bare
  #<UNDEFINED-MODULE-ERROR> before).  REQUIRE of a missing module now prints the
  module name, the modules directory that was searched (new BOCL:*MODULES-
  DIRECTORY*, set by the boot scan), the modules that WERE found there, and —
  for ASDF specifically — the exact commands to build and install it.

To actually get (require :asdf) working on an install:
    git submodule update --init modules/asdf-src
    make modules && make install     # needs a host CL (sbcl/ccl/clisp) to build
                                      # ASDF's single-file release
Then `(require :asdf)` loads it (slowly).  See modules/README.org.

Still rough (tracked separately, NOT blockers for this issue):
- Interpreter speed: loading asdf.lisp takes minutes; apropos over the image is
  ~20 s (see slow-apropos.issue).
- Making ASDF turnkey without the manual submodule step is a packaging decision
  (committing the 700 KB generated asdf.lisp vs. the current build-from-submodule
  design); left as-is per modules/README.org.

======================================================================
REAL ROOT CAUSE & FIX (v1.4.33)
-------------------------------
The v1.4.32 diagnosis was incomplete.  The actual reason (require :asdf) /
(require :factorial) failed even after a correct `make install` is that the
MODULES DIRECTORY was derived from where the PRELUDE happened to be found at
runtime, and prelude-finding is CWD-dependent:

  find_prelude() (main.c) uses program_path = argv[0].  When bocl is run by bare
  name ("bocl"), argv[0] has no '/', so the exe-relative search strips it to "."
  (the CURRENT directory) and tries "./bootstrap/prelude.lisp".  pjb runs bocl
  from inside the source checkout (~/src/public/bocl), so that path EXISTS and
  wins over the installed /opt/local/libexec/bocl/prelude.lisp.  The boot scan
  then looked in <that dir>/modules/ = ~/src/public/bocl/bootstrap/modules/,
  which does not exist (built modules live in <repo>/modules/ and installed ones
  in <prefix>/libexec/bocl/modules/).  Hence "Modules found there: none", even
  though the modules were installed correctly.  `which bocl` = /opt/local/bin/bocl
  is a red herring: argv[0] stays "bocl" and the real exe path is never recovered.

Fix (per the agreed design — do NOT locate resources from the executable path):
the modules directory is now resolved by a kernel primitive,
%DEFAULT-MODULES-DIRECTORY, INDEPENDENT of argv[0]/CWD/where the prelude was
found:
  1. $BOCL_MODULES_DIR                         (explicit runtime override)
  2. <dir of the compiled-in BOCL_PRELUDE_PATH>/modules/  — BOCL_PRELUDE_PATH is
     set by `make install` to $(bocllibexecdir)/prelude.lisp, so it carries the
     install PREFIX (e.g. /opt/local/libexec/bocl/modules/).
  3. a last-resort default (/opt/local/libexec/bocl/modules/).
The boot scan (prelude) now calls this instead of (directory-namestring
*load-truename*)/modules/.  BOCL:*MODULES-DIRECTORY* holds the resolved value.

Verified: with no env, the dir resolves to the install path regardless of CWD;
$BOCL_MODULES_DIR overrides it; pointing it at <repo>/modules/ makes
(require :factorial) load and (factorial:fact 10) => 3628800.

FUTURE (not done, deliberately kept simple now): have `make install` compile a
generated install-paths module so a `make install PREFIX=/foo/bar` that differs
from the build-time PREFIX still bakes the right paths; and consult XDG dirs
before the compiled-in default.

The FUTURE point above seems to be implemented, but let's ammend it;
it seems the prelude is searched in these 3 places by default:

/opt/local/libexec/bocl/prelude.lisp
/usr/local/libexec/bocl/prelude.lisp
/usr/libexec/bocl/prelude.lisp

Let's go and search the modules in the same 3 places by default.

/opt/local/libexec/bocl/modules/
/usr/local/libexec/bocl/modules/
/usr/libexec/bocl/modules/

Then this issue will be complete, and can be moved to issues/closed.
